A||||3||Cheever2||Duke rebuild/replacement questions||||6||
Z||000000||Cheever2||12-28-2000||08:59 PM||||Searching didn't tell me what I wanted to know, thus new thread, and maybe it will help someone else too someday.<BR>Have a 88 Duke Coupe and it runs good 'nuff to get me places, but to prepare for the future I want to get a replacement ready.  That way I can take my time with it, use the experience to bond with my boys, and spend $ only when I'm ready to.  So, before you start trying to talk me into using some other power source, first: I'm cheap; and second: I'm clueless regarding anything that has to do with the ECM stuff.  When the time comes, I want to take one motor out, put another one in, reconneck everything and start'er up (sure it never happens that way but u know what I mean).  If that can be done with a *relatively* inexpensive alternative to the Duke, I'd like to know about it.<P>Otherwise, my questions are: <BR>What parts should be replaced with new ones?<BR>What do I take to a machine shop, and what do I ask for?<BR>What higher performance can I get considering the aforementioned lack of computer savvy?<BR>What sources can you recommend for anything which I can't get just any ol' place?<BR>What else should be done while I'm at it, even tho the car is in fairly good shape?  <P>I would like to make the engine, engine bay and underside really pretty for shows, if you have any tips on that.<P>I don't expect volumes of answers to miraculously appear. I'll learn as I go, but you folks are great and I knew this would be the best place to start.  TIA<P>||||reg||6
Z||000001||Cooter||12-28-2000||09:29 PM||||The basic header would be a good add on and a free flowing exhaust. Somewhere (theogre- I think) had some info about a different year model of the Duke that was more powerful. Maybe that would be your best starting point- find which engine had the most power to begin with and then work from there. You could always free up some extra horses by the traditional port/polish on the intake and head. Somewhere, there is a set of high performance pistons that would work in the Duke, just ask your machine shop to find them.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||12-28-2000||10:10 PM||||Have you checked Ira Crummey's site?<BR> <A HREF="http://www.homestead.com/Fiero2M4/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.homestead.com/Fiero2M4/index.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-28-2000||11:51 PM||||If you want to stay 4 cyl you can use any available engine from 87-91. The 2.5l engine is also found in Grand Am and Olds Cutlass Calais. You can use either the straight block or the balancer motor. The 2 motors are the same as far as the ECM and everything else go. The Ballancer is supposed to idle a little better but the straight block has a regular spin on oil filter that is a little less of a problem to change.<P>Note that if you use a cutlass or grand am motor you'll need to change the pullies and the serpent belt tensioner to Fiero parts.<P>Since this means changing the waterpump pully you might as well replace the water pump as well. <P>Used Water pumps have a nasty habit of failing if they are left sitting for a long time. Usually they fail a week or a month after you start running them again.<P>You might as well learn about port and polish. that's cheap HP for any motor. There is a very good guide about it here--&gt; <A HREF="http://www.sa-motorsports.com/" TARGET=_blank>Standard Abrasives Motor Sports Division</A><P><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-28-2000).]||||reg||1
